  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03404v1</id>
    <title>VidDub: Replacement-Aware Distillation for Two-Step Video Dubbing</title>
    <updated>2026-11-02T16:33:09Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03404v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.03404v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Video dubbing aims to synthesize lip movements that match target speech while preserving the speaker identity, head pose, and background of a source video. Existing editors are predominantly trained via self-reconstruction under original paired audio, even though the source mouth becomes a conflicting target when driving speech is replaced. We introduce replacement-aware cross-audio supervision: paired-audio samples use full-frame reconstruction, whereas unpaired replacement-audio samples mask out source-mouth reconstruction losses and supervise the mouth solely through audio-visual synchronization while preserving unmasked facial regions and background. Because offline dubbing provides the complete source video and target speech beforehand, we present VidDub, a full-context diffusion editor distilled into two denoising steps (2 NFE). Our replacement-aware teacher first learns accurate lip synchronization without regressing toward conflicting source mouth movements. Matched trajectory regression then transfers the teacher's CFG-guided trajectories to a two-step student, and distribution matching distillation recovers visual quality after step compression. We will release the curated training dataset, VidDub-Bench, fixed paired- and cross-audio evaluation manifests, preprocessing outputs, and unified evaluation code. Across three training seeds, masking out conflicting source-mouth reconstruction improves cross-audio Sync-C by 0.833; the final two-step model combines strong lip synchronization with superior visual quality, identity preservation, and visual speech recognition across four benchmarks.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-02T16:33:09Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>https://github.com/LAW1223/VidDub</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Yexin Liu</name>
    </author>
