  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.02660v1</id>
    <title>HeLM: Hierarchical Explicit Language Memory for Non-Markovian Manipulation</title>
    <updated>2026-11-02T07:20:12Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.02660v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.02660v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Recent advances in Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models have enabled fine manipulation capabilities, yet performing tasks that require working memory remains a key challenge. Most existing VLA architectures operate under a Markovian assumption, mapping only the current observation to an action, which leads to failure in non-Markovian environments. Inspired by the human 'inner monologue', we propose Hierarchical Explicit Language Memory (HeLM), a hierarchical VLA framework that maintains task-relevant history in explicit language through factorized event detection and event-triggered memory updates. We evaluate HeLM across 10 non-Markovian real-world tasks with 56 variants, including five newly formulated inter-episode tasks that require persistent context across task boundaries. HeLM improves intra-episode success by 12.8 percentage points over the strongest baseline and achieves 78% subtask success on compositional generalization tasks, while using up to 4.5x less memory and running 2.5x faster than the keyframe baseline at long horizons.</summary>
    <category term="cs.RO" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-02T07:20:12Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>32 pages, 14 figures, including supplementary material. Project page: https://kimspacehuni.github.io/HeLM/</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.RO"/>
    <author>
      <name>Gyeonghun Kim</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Donghoon Kim</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Minsoo Choi</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Suyun Lee</name>
    </author>
