  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05304v1</id>
    <title>LoopVQ: Sub 1-bit KV Cache Quantization for Looped Transformers</title>
    <updated>2026-11-03T18:16:29Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05304v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.05304v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Looped transformers are a parameter-efficient alternative to standard transformers for language modeling. By reapplying the same layers across multiple loop steps, recent looped language models match the performance of non-looped models several times their size. While this architecture effectively reduces parameter memory, each loop step still stores its own keys and values, so the key-value (KV) cache grows linearly with the number of loop steps and becomes the main memory bottleneck. We observe that the KV cache of looped models is highly redundant along the loop axis and that the loop steps differ substantially in their sensitivity to quantization. Based on these observations, we propose $\textbf{LoopVQ}$, a KV cache vector quantization method for looped transformers that codes channels of multiple loop steps jointly, and fine-tunes the codebooks to match the output of the full-precision model. At 0.5 bits per element, LoopVQ outperforms existing KV cache vector quantization methods for standard LLMs on general, long chain-of-thought reasoning, and long-context retrieval tasks with Ouro and Huginn models. Its design is orthogonal to these methods, and combined with TaSQ, it retains much of the BF16 accuracy even at 0.25 bits per element. We also implement LoopVQ in SGLang, where it nearly halves the decode latency at a 32K-token context and raises the peak serving throughput by up to $4.3\times$ and $6.1\times$ on the 1.4B and 2.6B Ouro models compared to the BF16 baseline.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-03T18:16:29Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Minsoo Cheong</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Sungjoo Yoo</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04386v1</id>
    <title>StepX-OPD: Rethinking Multi-Teacher On-Policy Distillation in Diffusion Models</title>
    <updated>2026-11-03T06:16:16Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04386v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.04386v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>A deployable text-to-image diffusion model must hold several capabilities at once, such as instruction following, text rendering and human preference, and must sample in a few steps. Capabilities and few-step sampling are built in separate stages: a full-step model first consolidates capabilities through multi-teacher on-policy distillation, then serves as a fixed teacher for step distillation. Every full-step model we evaluate scores lower once compressed, and the second stage cannot begin until the first finishes. We introduce StepX-OPD, which merges the two stages: one student acquires every capability while learning to sample in a few steps, so frozen specialists supervise it on the states its own rollout visits rather than on a longer trajectory it never runs. Because a decomposition of the rollout's KL weights velocity mismatch most heavily near the clean end, we also regress the student's velocity on the low-noise part of the rollout. Separately, we find that capability integration makes models write prompt words into images on prompts that never asked for text, and we give a variant that lowers it. We evaluate on SD3.5-Medium and FLUX.1-dev across instruction following, text rendering, image quality and human preference. On both architectures, StepX-OPD attains the highest normalized average score among the four-step methods compared and exceeds the forty-step Flow-OPD model, and on SD3.5-Medium it trains with about 37% fewer GPU-hours than sequential DiffusionOPD followed by step distillation.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-03T06:16:16Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Chenru Wang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Xuanyang Zhang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yucheng Han</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Wenbo Nie</name>
    </author>
    <author>
