  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03768v1</id>
    <title>Multi-layer Indexer: Overlapping Selection and Attention in DeepSeek Sparse Attention</title>
    <updated>2026-11-02T15:46:01Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03768v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.03768v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Sparse attention cuts the cost of long-context inference by making the attention matrix sparse: each query attends only a subset of the sequence, chosen by a fixed pattern or, in recent production LLMs, learned per query. In DeepSeek Sparse Attention (DSA), the attention of DeepSeek-V3.2 and the GLM-5 family, every layer is preceded by a lightning indexer that scores the whole cache and keeps the k=2048 highest-scoring tokens for the layer to attend. At long context this indexer scan, not the sparse attention it feeds, is where the decode time goes. But adjacent layers select nearly the same tokens, so a layer can attend the selection of the previous indexer layer instead of its own. This removes the dependency: each indexer then runs concurrently with the attention of its layer. The resulting Multi-layer Indexer (MLI) needs no retraining and no new weights. Its default realization changes no kernel, running inside SGLang's production decode graph; an optional fused decode kernel accelerates it further on DeepSeek-V3.2. MLI composes with IndexCache (the cross-layer reuse GLM-5.2 ships as IndexShare): IndexCache drops the indexer from some layers, and MLI hides the scan on the rest. We serve DeepSeek-V3.2 and GLM-5 under IndexCache's protocol at retentions 1/2 to 1/32 (the fraction of layers that keep an indexer) and contexts 10K to 195K. At equal retention the composition decodes faster than IndexCache: up to 14.5% faster on DeepSeek-V3.2 and 9.8% on GLM-5 at 1/2, and 7.9% and 5.1% at 1/4, largest where IndexCache keeps the most indexers and growing with context. At retention 1/r it is faster still than IndexCache at 1/(2r) while keeping twice the fresh selections: at 128K it reaches 1.32x the unmodified model's decode throughput at 1/4, above IndexCache's 1.28x at 1/8. Its LongBench, RULER and LongBench v2 accuracy stays within a few percentage points of IndexCache's.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-02T15:46:01Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>20 pages (8 pages main text), 4 figures, 11 tables. Accepted to the Long-Context Foundation Models (LCFM) Workshop at NeurIPS 2026, Atlanta, December 12, 2026. Code: https://github.com/ArjunKrishnak/dsa-multi-layer-indexer</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Serdarcan Dilbaz</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Arjun Krishna</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Kiran Kamble</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Daniel M. Bikel</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07613v1</id>
    <title>Towards Real-Time Full-Waveform LiDAR Transformers via Intensity-Guided Token Reduction and Physics-Aware Augmentation</title>
    <updated>2026-11-05T07:56:01Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07613v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.07613v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>LiDAR is a critical sensor for autonomous driving and robotics, yet it remains vulnerable to adverse conditions such as fog, rain, transparent objects, and low-reflectance surfaces. Full-waveform LiDAR (FWL) addresses these limitations by capturing the complete return waveform as a histogram, preserving rich reflection characteristics including multi-path echoes and weak signals. While recent Transformer-based approaches have demonstrated promise on FWL data, two fundamental bottlenecks remain: high computational cost from redundant tokens in low-intensity background regions, and limited generalization due to scarce annotated training data. We address both bottlenecks by exploiting the physical structure inherent to FWL data. First, we propose FWL-ToPM, a token pruning and merging mechanism that leverages intensity distributions to selectively reduce tokens while retaining critical waveform content. Second, we introduce FWLAug, a data augmentation framework designed as a regularization method that preserves waveform structure, retaining the Time-of-Flight waveform of each ray and the frustum geometry, to improve generalization to unseen scenes. Evaluated on the Ghost-FWL benchmark, FWL-ToPM achieves over 8 times speedup at real-time throughput alongside a nearly 9-point F1 gain over the prior state of the art, establishing the first real-time FWL Transformer that simultaneously improves accuracy. FWLAug further improves performance on both the standard benchmark and an out-of-distribution test set of unseen scenes. Together, these methods push FWL-based perception toward practical deployment. The code and the OOD dataset are publicly available and can be accessed via the project page: https://keio-csg.github.io/FWL-ToPM</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-05T07:56:01Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Accepted to NeurIPS 2026 (Main)</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Kotaro Oishi</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Kazuma Ikeda</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Ryosei Hara</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Ryo Yoshida</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Mariko Isogawa</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Kentaro Yoshioka</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.34074v1</id>
    <title>Identically Trained Language Models Break on Different Jailbreaks</title>
    <updated>2026-10-25T05:30:02Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.34074v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.34074v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Jailbreak benchmarks test one checkpoint and treat which jailbreaks break it as a property of its pretraining data and safety training. We ask whether another pretraining run of the same data, given the same safety training, would break on the same jailbreaks. For each of 25 pretraining data recipes, we take 1B base models from 3 pretraining seeds and give each the same safety post-training with 2 post-training seeds, for 150 models in all. We then collect 20 responses from every model to a shared set of jailbreak prompts and correlate the per-prompt non-refusal rates between every pair of models. On average, two post-trained copies of the same pretraining run have per-prompt non-refusal rates that correlate at 0.984. A different pretraining seed lowers the correlation to 0.811, and a different data recipe lowers it only slightly further, to 0.779. These results indicate that which jailbreaks break a checkpoint depends much more on its pretraining run than on its data, a pattern that holds in all 25 data recipes and at 150M and 300M parameters. In practice, the best of 91 attack templates found on one checkpoint stays the best after a new pretraining seed in only 27% of model pairs, against 67% after a new post-training seed. Each pretraining run also leaves a fingerprint: across 3,211 prompts, a model's per-prompt non-refusal rates identify its pretraining run among all 75 runs every time, and a random subset of 50 prompts still does so 98% of the time.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-25T05:30:02Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
      <name>Chi-Wei Chang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Mu-Tien Kuo</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Kuan-Wei Lee</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Meng-Hsun Tsai</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.36544v1</id>
    <title>Monitor Accuracy Isn't the Answer: A Decision-Value Framework for Evaluating AI Agent Restart Monitors</title>
    <updated>2026-10-27T02:59:21Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.36544v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.36544v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Classification accuracy and true-positive and false-positive rates alone do not determine whether restarting an AI agent improves success. Using an exact alarm-to-action identity for an independent clean restart with zero monitoring overhead, we show analytically that a monitor with fixed 90% accuracy can swing from a five-percentage-point loss to a 35-point gain in success rate, depending solely on the replacement attempt's residual-budget success probability. We introduce two techniques for evaluating monitors and progress signals in decision terms. First, a decision-sufficiency criterion, a binary-action specialization of Blackwell's comparison of experiments, identifies which progress representations or context compressions preserve the information needed to choose optimally between the specified actions, independent of trace reconstruction fidelity. Second, a finite-sample, model-free decision audit uses simultaneous binomial intervals over existing agent traces, without additional model calls, to certify a preference for "restart" or "continue" under stated sampling assumptions, or return "abstain" when evidence is insufficient. In a study of 194 externally evaluated model executions on 19 selected SymPy issues from SWE-bench Verified, with a retrospective audit of 130 of these traces, we find a 2.29-point apparent restart gain traced to a single surviving failed attempt, a 42.29-point loss on a second model, no positive replication of the initial gain on prospective task cohorts, and zero of 103 live comparisons meeting the specified certification bar. These results motivate evaluating agent monitors through recovery opportunity, decision value, and statistical support, rather than classification performance alone.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.IR" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-27T02:59:21Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>29 pages, 6 figures, 14 tables</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Siddharth Jain</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Venkat N. Vedam</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40516v1</id>
    <title>How I Aligned Your Model: Scaling Up Multilingual Safety Training with Templates</title>
    <updated>2026-10-29T17:26:02Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40516v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40516v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Safety alignment for Large Language Models is heavily focused on English with limited multilingual representation, particularly damaging low-resource language performance. Related open training datasets are often limited in size or do not follow region-localized harmful topics to broaden the scope of alignment beyond cultures. We propose a scalable template-based augmentation pipeline to produce massive multilingual refusal and benign corpora, extracting safe templates from harmful prompts, translating them and then filling in the slots with culturally authentic entities, both harmful and benign. As a result, we release MSafeTemp, a validated corpus spanning 60 languages useful for multilingual safety alignment and evaluation. Using the dataset, we show that English-only evaluation misjudges multilingual safety: for several models, attacks that fail in English succeed once translated, while culturally localized variants expose further failures that translation alone misses. We also confirm that augmenting safety training data through templates allows for higher diversity and quality of alignment, resulting in improved safety performance beyond English. MSafeTemp and the respective pipeline are aimed at facilitating research and development of safer and more responsible models for less-represented cultures.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-29T17:26:02Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
