  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03330v1</id>
    <title>The SIMPLE Law for Energy Cost of LLM Inference</title>
    <updated>2026-11-02T15:51:46Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03330v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.03330v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Generative AI is billed per token, yet tokens are not equally expensive. Each token enters the KV-cache and raises the cost of those that follow, so energy grows non-linearly along a sequence whose tokens look identical. No analytical model states what a token costs, and the most accurate SOTA model is a fit, not a law: it absorbs architecture into fitted coefficients, requiring per-LLM fitting. We derive closed-form expressions of the computational and memory-access complexity of LLM inference in terms of architectural hyperparameters, covering MHA and GQA, and compare a family of analytical models of increasing granularity. From these, we obtain the SIMPLE Inference Model to Predict LLMs Energy (SIMPLE), a four-parameter law that separates prefill from decode, and decode into KV-cache cost and next-token computation. Over traces of 13 dense LLMs from six families on NVIDIA H100 GPUs, it attains R2 = 0.969 and 11.97% MAPE, 3.1-3.5x below the SOTA, without per-LLM calibration. The SIMPLE law explains as well as predicts: prefill energy grows quadratically with the hidden dimension, so larger LLMs suffer most from long inputs; and more aggressive GQA KV grouping reduces the per-token energy by up to 3x relative to standard MHA. Increasing the reasoning token budget pushes output length beyond the analytically derived energy minimum, driving super-linear energy growth; although accuracy grows with the budget, energy grows faster, so Intelligence per Joule peaks at an intermediate budget and falls by up to 3.14x across a 4.0x reasoning-budget range. Agentic serving raises energy per request by 13.0x relative to non-agentic ones, driven by the growth of both the prefill and the KV-cache terms with input length; while prefix caching removes most of the prefill cost (-93%), the KV-cache term is unaffected, so even a cached agentic request costs 4.8x more.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.PF" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-02T15:51:46Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Submitted to Future Generation Computer Systems</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Hiari Pizzini Cavagna</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Andrea Proia</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Daniele Cesarini</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Zeynep Kiziltan</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Andrea Bartolini</name>
