  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08237v1</id>
    <title>Terminal-style Mathematical Modeling Bench: The First Agentic and Verifiable Benchmark for Mathematical Modeling</title>
    <updated>2026-11-05T16:18:35Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08237v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.08237v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Mathematical modeling turns an open-ended real-world problem into assumptions, a model, a solution, and a defended recommendation, and its answers matter only if they are both close to a credible reference and physically feasible; existing benchmarks either score solver fragments or grade reports with an LLM judge, leaving those numbers unchecked. We introduce TB-MathModel (the Terminal-style Mathematical Modeling Bench), the first benchmark to make complete contest-style modeling problems agentic, verifiable, and terminal at once, over 18 real CUMCM and MCM problems (2023--2025). Each task ships the verbatim statement, official attachments, and figures in a containerized terminal; the agent returns one JSON artifact, scored on the numeric core of the final question against a reproduction of the Outstanding-award paper. Two design choices distinguish it: direction-aware, one-sided scoring, which never penalises beating the anchor, and two-layer feasibility verification---a programmatic gate over six decidable invariant forms plus 624 Lean~4 obligations---with a hard gate that zeroes infeasible answers. Each task is scored against a panel of human outstanding papers rather than one, so a model must be close to many accepted answers; across seven multimodal entries (three seeds each) the strongest reach a multi-reference O-Eval of 64.58\%, while the \$2.54 open model holds 59.81\% at $1/100$ the cost. TB-MathModel turns the numeric core of modeling into a reproducible, machine-checkable signal for measuring long-horizon modeling competence and for collecting agent trajectories. The source code is available at https://github.com/wxj630/terminal-bench-math-modeling.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-05T16:18:35Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>20 pages, 4 figures, 14 tables</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
      <name>Xiaojun Wu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Cehao Yang</name>
    </author>
