  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06498v1</id>
    <title>Signed but Steerable: Measuring and Closing the Text-Vector Integrity Gap in Offline Retrieval-Augmented Generation</title>
    <updated>2026-11-04T13:32:04Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06498v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.06498v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Offline retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) on edge devices is updated through signed knowledge packs that carry text and precomputed embedding vectors. A signature proves who published a pack, not that its vectors encode its text. We study this text-vector integrity gap. A verifier that recomputes vectors and accepts them within a cosine tolerance concedes a predictable share of queries to an attacker who controls the vectors: the hijack rate equals the distribution of angular retrieval margins at the tolerance (the margin law). Margins are small in five corpora and two embedding models, so an idealised tolerance at the level of floating-point noise leaves up to 5.5% of queries exposed (at most 1.2% in any corpus once exact duplicate documents are excluded), and a threshold calibrated from our CPU INT8-FP32 comparison of a small embedding model (at the empirical 0.1% quantile of honest cosines) leaves 98-100% exposed; on a Raspberry Pi 5 running a local language model, such an attack lowers correct answers from 0.75 to 0.39. Integrity audits of compressed vector stores fare no better: on a FAISS product-quantisation index over 57,638 documents, tampered vectors that all pass the audit put the attacker's document first for 25% of targets. Verifying quantisation codes instead, with ambiguity decided by the verifier, a calibrated guard band and canonicalisation, accepted every honest vector in our samples on three platforms and left the publisher no choice. We also show that common floating-point and dynamic-INT8 inference pipelines are not bit-reproducible across batch sizes or architectures, and report pre-registered negative results on abstention, context pruning and semantic screening. We release code and data.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CR" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.IR" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-04T13:32:04Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>20 pages, 2 figures, 7 tables. Code and data: https://github.com/securagenai/signed-but-steerable (DOI 10.5281/zenodo.23948839)</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CR"/>
    <author>
      <name>Georgios Feretzakis</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Alexandros Papaspyridis</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06768v1</id>
    <title>PyRUA-REAL: Robot Exploration and Adaptive Learning through RSI in the Real World</title>
    <updated>2026-11-04T16:46:03Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06768v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.06768v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Coding agents powered by models such as GPT-6-Astra connect spatial reasoning with executable robot behavior, raising the question of how such agents can produce lasting improvements in robot capabilities from physical interaction. In this work, we study embodied recursive self-improvement (RSI) through real-world exploration, code refinement, and deployment using PyRUA-REAL, a framework in which coding agents retain interaction experience as persistent memory and reusable executable skills that inform subsequent exploration and improvement. Our empirical study places physical manipulation at the center of the improvement process, examining how agents collaborate, what kinds of execution feedback support effective code refinement, and how task specifications and previously acquired skills influence robustness and task transfer. We further investigate the efficiency and capability of the resulting programs when deployed without involving an online agent for decision-making. Across eight real-world tasks, PyRUA-REAL achieves an average success rate of 91.1%. In complementary evaluation on 42 tasks from the RoboDojo benchmark, it achieves an average success rate of 84.4%. This study provides empirical guidance for turning physical exploration into reusable robot skills that support continued improvement and robust, efficient deployment.</summary>
    <category term="cs.RO" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-04T16:46:03Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.RO"/>
    <author>
      <name>Qiang Wang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jianxin Bi</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Shulong Jiang</name>
    </author>
