  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40119v1</id>
    <title>SynthFaces: Balanced Large Scale Human Dataset</title>
    <updated>2026-10-29T14:17:06Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40119v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40119v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>The pursuit of fair and equitable AI in human-centric tasks is critically hindered by the inherent biases and privacy risks of real-world datasets, which offer imbalanced representations of identity and often contain sensitive personal information. Consequently, state-of-the-art generative models trained on this skewed data exhibit unfair performance, particularly in identity-preserving generation. In this work we propose a pipeline for controllable generation of dataset with diverse faces for generative models training and finetuning. We achieve this by expanding foundational Arc2Face diffusion model with a flow matching generative model in the manifold of identity embeddings. We demonstrate that such approach achieves both goals of generative fidelity and label diversity not present in real human-centric datasets. As a result we collect and further contextualize a highly balanced identity-centric dataset of humans named SynthFaces. By design, our released images and identities are synthetic, reducing direct reliance on real personal images; we empirically check identity separation with a nearest-neighbor non-leakage metric. We purpose it for training and tuning of fair models for various downstream tasks, and showcase its usability in finetuning an existing adapter-based method for personalized generation.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-29T14:17:06Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>accepted at BMVC 2026 github: https://github.com/ControlGenAI/SynthFaces</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Sergey Karpukhin</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Denis Bobkov</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Kirill Morozov</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Aibek Alanov</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.41104v1</id>
    <title>MUON-RT Reusing the Teacher's Trajectory for Momentum-Free Muon for LLM Self-Distillation</title>
    <updated>2026-10-30T00:50:49Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.41104v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.41104v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Self-distillation methods for large language models with an exponential moving average (EMA) teacher already retain a history of student updates. Muon nevertheless stores a separate momentum buffer, increasing persistent training memory. We propose Muon with Reused Teacher (Muon-RT), which reuses the teacher-student displacement to eliminate momentum buffers for hidden weight matrices. We show that this displacement encodes an exponentially weighted history of applied updates and smooths temporally uncorrelated noise under a local model. Muon-RT removes its radial component, normalizes it spectrally, and mixes it with the current gradient after Newton-Schulz (NS) orthogonalization. Under explicit regularity and alignment assumptions, we establish linear contraction of an expected objective-gap bound toward a residual error level. With Self-Distillation Fine-Tuning (SDFT) on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, Muon-RT improves average accuracy over AdamW by 2.6 points with 42% less persistent memory, and over momentum Muon by 5.4 points with 27% less persistent memory. With Self-Distillation Policy Optimization (SDPO) on Olmo-3-7B-Instruct, it improves average accuracy over AdamW by 2.0 points.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="stat.ML" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-30T00:50:49Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Under Submission</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Aryan Das</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Vinay Kumar Verma</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Aakansha Mishra</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.37739v1</id>
    <title>OpenFrontiers: A Collection of Open Research Problems and a Benchmark for Scientific Rediscovery</title>
    <updated>2026-10-27T16:49:33Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.37739v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.37739v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Large language models (LLMs) have shown increasing promise in research-level problem solving, yet a comprehensive collection of such problems is still missing, and evaluating them on research-level problems remains challenging. We introduce OpenFrontiers, a cross-disciplinary resource that combines a structured corpus of open research problems with a retrospective benchmark of recently resolved problems in mathematics. The corpus records problem statements, context, progress, and other information for 12,151 problems. The benchmark contains 200 problems, 110 resolved in 2026 and 90 still open, each with a Lean~4 statement checked by automated probes and two independent reviews; a subset HQ-50 further filters based on quality score, etc. Our evaluation protocol grades formal proofs with two independent checkers in an isolated sandbox under closed-book, frozen-retrieval, and live-web conditions. Evaluating seven LLM agents on the HQ-50 subset under a standardized budget, we find that closed-book rediscovery is rare, at most 7 of 40 resolved problems, whereas live-web success mostly reflects retrieval of published solutions. By linking a broad research-problem corpus to a formally graded evaluation subset with documented solutions, OpenFrontiers provides a foundation for measuring research-level rediscovery and studying progress toward solving currently open scientific problems. The dataset and benchmark could be found in https://huggingface.co/collections/FlippyDora/openfrontiers.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-27T16:49:33Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Jiarui Yao</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Ruida Wang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Tong Zhang</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01120v1</id>
    <title>StreamDuo: A Live Commentator and a Research Agent for Unseen Games</title>
    <updated>2026-10-31T17:08:13Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01120v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.01120v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Streaming video LLMs can now commentate live gameplay, but they are trained and tested on the same games. We study cross-game streaming commentary, in which a model trained on one game commentates games it has never seen, and find that current commentators transfer their timing but not their knowledge of the game world. We propose StreamDuo, which pairs a trainable commentator with a frozen research agent that reads the wiki of the game being played. When the commentator sees something it cannot name, it sends the current frames, and the research agent returns a few wiki entries two seconds later while the stream continues. We train only the commentator, on a single game, in two stages. Information-gain labels teach it to ask only where an answer would help it say what the human caster said, and on-policy self-distillation from a copy of itself that sees the ideal answer teaches it to use noisy answers. Trained on Baldur's Gate 3 and tested on ten unseen games, StreamDuo raises entity recall from 0.082 to 0.116 over Proact-VL trained on the same data, cuts source-game leakage from 9.9% to 1.0%, and achieves the best timing and the highest judged liveliness and overall quality among streaming models.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-31T17:08:13Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>18 pages, 7 figures</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Zixian Gao</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Penghao Luo</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Xiangcheng Cao</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Lixin Xiu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Ziqi Cai</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Zhen Li</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Erwin Wu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Kaipeng Zhang</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08449v1</id>
    <title>VFlash: Fast, Accurate Visual Planning via Flash Primitive Decoding</title>
    <updated>2026-11-05T18:44:43Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08449v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.08449v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Models for physical AI tasks, such as robotics and autonomous driving, need not reason solely in language. They can use visual plans--points, bounding boxes, and trajectories--to represent spatial goals and anticipate motion. However, autoregressive vision-language models generate these plans one coordinate token at a time, making richer predictions increasingly costly. We introduce VFlash, a method for fast, accurate visual planning through Flash Primitive Decoding. VFlash exploits the known structure of visual primitives to predict their coordinates jointly, using a lightweight drafter that generates an entire primitive in one forward pass. The layout adapts to the primitive's type, dimensionality, and length, supporting compact boxes and long, variable-length trajectories. Crucially, spatial accuracy need not require reproducing an autoregressive model's exact token sequence. VFlash therefore trains on annotated primitives and directly accepts structurally valid predictions without target-token agreement. Across the evaluated 2D and 3D robot trajectory tasks, VFlash consistently reduces prediction errors relative to autoregressive decoding. Preliminary single-rollout measurements indicate approximately 25x faster generation for 3D point motion forecasting. A world action model conditioned on VFlash plans achieves 70.2% average success across four MolmoSpaces task suites, compared with 62.6% for the baseline.</summary>
    <category term="cs.RO" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-05T18:44:43Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.RO"/>
    <author>
      <name>Yu-Cheng Chou</name>
    </author>
    <author>
