  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05376v1</id>
    <title>From Autonomous Agents to Cognitive Symbionts: Toward AI-Augmented Human Intelligence</title>
    <updated>2026-11-03T18:59:52Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05376v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.05376v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>For a decade, progress in artificial intelligence has been measured by how much a machine can do on its own. Large foundation models now write code and run multistep tasks, and personal agents act on a person's behalf. Yet people still forget what they once knew, miss what they are not looking at, fail to find the right words, and face hard choices without the benefit of their own history, because the machine that could help stands outside the situation. As AI moves into the physical world, attention has settled on one kind of intelligent individual, the embodied robot; we argue that the era of Physical AI holds a second kind of equal importance, the person augmented by AI. Alongside artificial general intelligence, which asks how intelligent a machine can be, stands AI-augmented human intelligence (AHI), which asks how much intelligence a machine can enable in a particular person. Realizing it calls for a new kind of system, a cognitive symbiont: a lifelong, embedded, real-time, private, low-friction, and proactive companion that senses what the person senses through external devices, keeps their context across years, models their situation and their mind, takes part in their thinking at the right moment, carries out everyday tasks with their context, and keeps learning the person as both change. We give the paradigm a conceptual core, the cognitive gap between what a person could bring to a situation and what they can hold in working memory, and organize its design space as a symbiosis loop of sensing, keeping, understanding, living alongside, acting for and thinking with the person, and growing, enclosed by a personal runtime and by trust. Because a symbiont changes what a person can do in front of others, it also raises risks of a new kind, from covert assistance in examinations to pressure on everyone to adopt it, and we treat their governance as part of the paradigm.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-03T18:59:52Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>59 pages, 21 figures, 18 tables. Project page: https://cognitive-symbiont.github.io</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
      <name>Hao Fei</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06796v1</id>
    <title>VA-TDM: A Unified View for Improving Few-Step Diffusion Models by Velocity Adversarial Distillation</title>
    <updated>2026-11-04T17:14:20Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06796v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.06796v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Few-step distillation offers efficient generation with diffusion and flow models, but preserving quality with only a few sampling steps remains challenging. Distribution-matching methods via score distillation provide a strong foundation, yet they rely on accurately tracking the student's changing distribution. At intermediate generation steps in few-step sampling, student-score estimation can be difficult, and commonly used estimators may describe a different distribution from the one used to update the student. Flow matching allows a more flexible approach to supervision through velocity fields constructed from the student's sampling process. Building on this perspective, we introduce Velocity Adversarial Distillation (VAD), which uses a learned critic to compare student and teacher fields and turn their disagreement into feedback for learning. VAD unifies score distillation, Fisher-based matching, and moment matching, also explaining existing heuristic MMD updates and delivering new training objectives. We further develop VA-TDM, which follows trajectory distribution matching: providing supervision for partial denoising along the student trajectory. Beyond this, VA-TDM combines transition-aware online velocity learning and dual-form VAD supervision across intermediate student transitions. Extensive results show that VA-TDM is powerful for learning few-step generators, achieving state-of-the-art four-step performance in both text-to-image and text-to-video generation across various backbones and scaling effectively to 33B MiniMax-H3. In particular, our VA-TDM-H3 notably outperforms the few-step Fast-H3 and is closer to the original many-step H3 according to human preference.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-04T17:14:20Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Project Page: https://luo-yihong.github.io/VA-TDM/ Code: https://github.com/Luo-Yihong/VA-TDM</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Yihong Luo</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Tianyu Pang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Tianyang Hu</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06728v1</id>
    <title>Turns Matter: Advancing Streaming Video RL via Turn-Level Credit</title>
    <updated>2026-11-04T16:16:27Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06728v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.06728v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Streaming video understanding requires models to repeatedly decide whether to respond or continue observing. Recently, Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) has been applied to this setting, with existing approaches emphasizing reward design while retaining a single trajectory-level advantage across turns. Such shared credit can reinforce redundant replies after earlier successes and discourage useful follow-ups after earlier failures. We propose TurnWise, a turn-level GRPO framework that aligns learning with individual streaming decisions. TurnWise addresses the mismatch between trajectory-level feedback and turn-level interaction, promoting informative responses and reducing redundancy. Specifically, turn-level credit assignment uses silence-completed prefix scoring to construct group-relative advantages from each decision's remaining return, separating its learning signal from earlier rewards. Turn-mean policy optimization averages token losses within each turn and then across turns, giving response and waiting decisions equal weight regardless of output length. These complementary designs improve streaming policy learning without a learned critic or additional rollouts. Extensive experiments show our method outperforms prior RL approaches for streaming video understanding.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-04T16:16:27Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>16 pages, 4 figures</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
