  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07909v1</id>
    <title>EDGAR: Efficient Diffusion-Driven Gaussian Avatar Refinement via Synthetic Failure</title>
    <updated>2026-11-05T12:25:51Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07909v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.07909v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Recent advances in Gaussian splatting have enabled efficient and photorealistic head avatar reconstruction, yet monocular video-based or image-based methods still suffer from under-constrained geometry and texture, leading to artifacts such as blurring, stretching, and temporal instability. We present EDGAR (Efficient Diffusion-Driven Gaussian Avatar Refinement), a conditional diffusion framework designed to enhance the quality of Gaussian avatars by correcting artifacts while preserving identity fidelity. To train EDGAR, we construct EDGAR-SET, a hybrid triplet dataset composed of real-captured and synthetic-rendered samples, which simulates realistic Gaussian avatar failure cases by pairing degraded renderings with ground-truth and reference images. EDGAR refines degraded frames through a fusion block that injects identity features extracted by the self-supervised Sapiens model into a one-step diffusion backbone, enabling effective artifact removal without sacrificing efficiency. Our EDGAR model incorporates a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architecture to address diverse degradation types and severity levels. The refined multi-view renderings are subsequently used as pseudo-targets to optimize a single underlying Gaussian avatar, so that the final avatar can be rendered without applying EDGAR to every output frame. Experiments on three different Gaussian avatar reconstruction methods, each evaluated under three different settings, show improvements in both perceptual and reconstruction measures over the raw outputs and an SDEdit-style refinement baseline. In addition, cross-view evaluation demonstrates improved calibrated consistency, while tests under large yaw and open-mouth expressions verify robustness under extreme settings. These results collectively indicate a useful operating regime for recoverable appearance artifacts. The code is available at https://kwanyun.github.io/edgar/</summary>
    <category term="cs.GR" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.MM" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-05T12:25:51Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Accepted to ACM Transactions on Graphics</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.GR"/>
    <author>
      <name>Kwan Yun</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Minhyeok Bang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Colette Suhjung Koo</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Junyong Noh</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04949v1</id>
    <title>HERIT: Enabling Global Access to Hanja-to-English Historical Translation by Mitigating Temporal Bias</title>
    <updated>2026-11-03T14:10:59Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04949v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.04949v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Historical archives are invaluable for multidisciplinary research but remain inaccessible globally due to severe language barriers. While manual translation is prohibitively expensive and time-consuming, simply applying existing machine translation models is inadequate due to unique linguistic nuances and the temporal bias inherent in scarce human-labeled corpora. To address these challenges, we propose HERIT, a systematic domain-adaptation pipeline that leverages retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) to generate high-quality pseudo-labeled data from abundant Hanja-Korean corpora. This approach substantially expands the training dataset, mitigating both data scarcity and temporal overfitting across different historical periods. Extensive automated and human evaluations demonstrate that HERIT consistently outperforms baselines. Finally, we translate previously untranslated portions of the archives using our model, thereby enabling global access to these resources for researchers.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-03T14:10:59Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Accepted to Findings of EMNLP 2026. 17 pages, 10 figures, 11 tables</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
      <name>Dayeon Jeong</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Kyeongpil Kang</name>
    </author>
    <arxiv:doi>10.18653/2026.findings-emnlp.2326</arxiv:doi>
    <link rel="related" href="https://doi.org/10.18653/2026.findings-emnlp.2326" title="doi"/>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.02075v1</id>
    <title>Decoupled Predictive Decision Making: A Shared Conditional Predictor for Agents</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T17:27:21Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.02075v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.02075v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Agent decisions depend on empirical relationships that deployment observations reveal and task descriptions alone may leave unresolved. We introduce Decoupled Predictive Decision Making (DPDM), an architecture that formulates heterogeneous agent decisions as conditional prediction with a shared pretrained learner. Language models provide semantic representations of current and historical records, while a general-purpose conditional predictor predicts task-defined targets from the represented evidence. Pretraining supplies reusable inference capability; deployment evidence instantiates problem-specific predictions without updating either model's neural parameters. Tool judgments, candidate preferences, and reasoning assessments share an evidence-and-query interface, with predictions mapped to choices by a decision rule. This architecture establishes complementary sources of agent capability: semantic knowledge acquired through language pretraining and empirical inference acquired through structured-data pretraining. A single frozen LimiX-2 predictor serves all five task categories. With Qwen3.5-27B as the semantic encoder, DPDM raises mean accuracy across 50 benchmarks from 78.80% with few-shot prompting to 84.92% under their respective evaluated history budgets, and reaches 82.33% with identical histories and queries. On ToolPRMBench's BFCL and ToolSandbox tasks, absolute accuracy gains are 34.26% and 28.87%, respectively, at the methods' respective evaluated history budgets. Accuracy improves as history grows. With identical imbalanced histories, mean balanced accuracy improves by an absolute 14.51% across five reasoning-step assessment tasks and four encoders. DPDM makes learning from experience an independently improvable foundation-model capability, creating a shared route for advances in conditional prediction to improve decisions across agent tasks.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-01T17:27:21Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>16 pages, 7 figures, 11 tables. Includes appendices</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Lang Mo</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Xingxuan Zhang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Qi Wang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Mingchao Hao</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Hao Yuan</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Chunjing Gan</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Wang Lu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Peng Cui</name>
    </author>
