  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03517v1</id>
    <title>Ask, Don't Equip: Question-Driven Coding Agents for Deep Kernel Optimization</title>
    <updated>2026-11-02T17:51:58Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03517v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.03517v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>LLM agents for GPU kernel optimization sit at two ends of a structure-freedom tradeoff. Scripted agents fix what the model does at each step, via a learned policy, an optimization menu, or fixed roles, and are sample-efficient where the recipe applies and stall where it does not. Open-ended agents let a self-directed model mutate a population of kernels and pay for the freedom in trials: thousands of evaluations, repeated edits, and wrong candidates. Both respond to hard targets by equipping the agent with more: parsed profiler output, disassembly analysis, retrieval over documentation and libraries, and stores of lessons. We argue that both lack direction, a hypothesis about where the remaining gap is, and that a short question asked at the right moment supplies it without narrowing what the agent may do next. We present KernelSeek, a question-driven agent that makes an existing CUDA kernel faster with no operator-specific knowledge and no faster implementation to imitate: a general-purpose coding agent, a minimal harness that enforces a verifiable goal, and questions at decision points, first from a human, then from a bank the agent applies to itself. On the same task, evaluator, hardware, and week, a tool-rich autonomous agent built in the prevailing style plateaued at 0.50x FlashAttention-3 after 133 model-driven steps; KernelSeek reached 1.13x. It then produced dependency-free FlashAttention forward kernels for Hopper and Blackwell, single CUDA files with no CUTLASS, CuTe, cuBLAS, or cuDNN, correct on all 182 scenarios per architecture and 1.16x FlashAttention-3 and 1.46x FlashAttention-4 (geometric mean). Across 162 human messages and 11.5K agent tool calls, every large gain followed a question that moved the agent to a different layer of the system or rejected a claimed limit, never one that supplied information. We release the kernels, harness, question bank, and all traces.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-02T17:51:58Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Gabriele Oliaro</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jaeseong Lee</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Zhaoyuan Su</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Sreeram Vennam</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Zhihao Jia</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Samyam Rajbhandari</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01926v1</id>
    <title>TAC-CoT: Tool-Empowered Implicit Chain-of-Thought via Interleaving Explicit Tokens</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T14:05:15Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01926v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.01926v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>LLM-based agentic systems are increasingly combined with CoT reasoning to solve tasks that require accurate reasoning, external knowledge, and multi-step deliberation. Yet, explicit CoT incurs substantial generation overhead, while implicit CoT, which replaces textual rationales with compact continuous tokens, does not naturally support the explicit communication interface required for agentic tool-use. We propose TAC-CoT, a tool-empowered adaptive implicit CoT framework that replaces explicit reasoning traces with compact latent reasoning segments while retaining textual tokens only for tool calls, observations, and final answers. This interleaved design enables agentic tool-use without verbalizing every intermediate reasoning step. Experiments on agentic math and QA benchmarks show that TAC-CoT improves accuracy by up to 8.8 points while reducing model forward passes by up to 6.8x. Furthermore, analyses show that TAC-CoT improves tool-use quality in both single- and multi-tool settings.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-01T14:05:15Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>EMNLP 2026 Main Long</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
      <name>Juseon Do</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Sangmin Bae</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jeesu Jung</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Hwanjun Song</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.00883v1</id>
    <title>Random Is Almost Enough? Stable and Low-Cost Benchmark Compression via BestRand</title>
    <updated>2026-10-31T12:36:58Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.00883v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.00883v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Benchmark compression reduces the often prohibitive time and cost of model evaluation by evaluating models on a small benchmark subset that closely matches full-benchmark results. To quantify estimation error, we derive an accurate analytical approximation to the error distribution when estimating full-benchmark scores from a subset, showing that most subsets yield small errors, while a few produce much larger errors, resulting in a long-tailed distribution. This means random sampling alone can achieve surprisingly strong performance with high probability, but remains unstable due to rare large errors. We further show that this instability is inherent to modern foundation models and persists even as more models are evaluated. Based on these findings, we introduce BestRand, a stable and low-cost benchmark compression method that preserves the efficiency of random sampling while substantially reducing its high-error tail. We validate BestRand on 70+ text, multimodal, and agent benchmarks, and further extend it to VLA evaluation. Compared with competitive methods, BestRand reduces high-error risk by 50.2% and estimation error by 16.4% on average, while requiring only 51.3% of the construction cost. It further reduces OOD estimation error by 21.8% on average across score-range, model-family, and temporal shifts. Together, our theory provides practical guidance for understanding and predicting the accuracy and reliability of benchmark compression, while BestRand enables stable and accurate compression at low cost. The code and data are publicly available onhttps://github.com/George-Ling3/BestRand.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-31T12:36:58Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>preprint</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
      <name>George Ling</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Shanshan Zhong</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Lijin Yang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Hao Yang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Nanxiang Li</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Zhongzhan Huang</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40131v3</id>
    <title>VideoMemBench: How Well Do MLLMs Remember in Video Conversations?</title>
    <updated>2026-11-03T14:34:07Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40131v3" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40131v3" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) can now process hour-long videos, making extended multi-turn dialogue over a single video a natural mode of use. Sustaining such a dialogue requires more than answering each question in isolation: the model must retrieve and reuse information established earlier in the conversation, both what it has seen and what it has said, an ability we term in-context video memory. Existing video QA benchmarks leave this ability unmeasured: their questions are mutually independent, never reference one another, and each is answerable from the video alone, so nothing ever requires carrying information across turns. We introduce VideoMemBench, in which each sample pairs a single video with a long multi-turn dialogue, whose turns are connected by typed cross-turn dependencies: an Episodic Dependency refers back to a visual episode described in an earlier turn, probing memory of what the model has seen, while an Answer Dependency builds on the model's own earlier answer, probing memory of what the model has said. Every dependency is verified considering two properties: 1) necessity, meaning the question cannot be answered without its earlier turns, and 2) non-leakage, meaning the question does not restate the information from its earlier turns. To enforce these properties, we design a construction pipeline with three LLM roles: a planner, a generator, and a judger, with each dialogue reviewed by human experts. VideoMemBench comprises 326 videos with 2,476 turns, of which 1,387 depend on earlier turns, including multi-parent dependencies and long-range call-back. Among over 20 evaluated open- and closed-source MLLMs, Claude Opus 5 performs the best with an overall score of only 45.7%, highlighting the challenge of in-context video memory. Our dataset and evaluation are released.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-29T14:22:51Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Zhengdong Hu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Chao Wang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Kaituo Feng</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Huanjin Yao</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Mingfei Lu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Hehe Fan</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yi Yang</name>
