  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05029v1</id>
    <title>DRAP: Dynamics-Risk Adaptive Plasticity for Continual Learning in Physical AI</title>
    <updated>2026-11-03T15:05:06Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05029v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.05029v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Continual learning in physical AI differs from conventional continual learning because small per-step policy errors can accumulate over a trajectory. Each deviation may move the agent outside the training distribution, increase subsequent prediction errors, and ultimately cause closed-loop task failure. This work introduces Dynamics-Risk Adaptive Plasticity (DRAP), a replay-free method that estimates the historical importance of adaptable model tensors from sample-level action gradients weighted by local trajectory-amplification risk. A ridge-regularized local linear model is fitted to expert state-action transitions, and the largest singular value of its state-transition matrix provides a policy-independent, Lyapunov-inspired estimate of one-step perturbation amplification. After each task, risk-weighted squared gradients are accumulated in a tensor-level importance memory. During subsequent tasks, DRAP uses normalized cumulative importance to scale the effective AdamW learning rate of each tensor, immediately reducing the plasticity of components that supported previous behavior at dynamically sensitive trajectory points. We instantiate DRAP on a fixed KappaTune-selected subnetwork of OpenVLA and evaluate replay-free sequential adaptation across the LIBERO Spatial, Object, and Goal suites. Compared with standard AdamW fine-tuning and EWC, the results show substantially improved retention of previously learned behaviors while preserving strong new-suite acquisition.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.RO" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-03T15:05:06Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Oswaldo Ludwig</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03852v1</id>
    <title>Why Pay for Long Reasoning When Rewrite Do Trick? Causal Effects of Reasoning History Compaction on Future Generation and Agent Cost</title>
    <updated>2026-11-02T19:21:48Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03852v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.03852v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Reasoning substantially improves language models' ability to solve difficult problems, but long reasoning traces make these gains expensive. Efficient reasoning should express useful analysis in fewer tokens by reducing repetition and text that adds little value to solving the problem. Progress in proprietary models motivates this goal, yet their hidden scratchpads limit direct study of how it is achieved. We investigate whether open-weight models can generate less verbose reasoning by changing the form of their own earlier thoughts, without retraining. Our intervention rewrites prior reasoning into compact state records before pending tool results are revealed, then compares continuations from original and compact histories. Across the tested models, accepted compact histories usually shorten future reasoning; a coding case study also shows less repeated verification after a shared candidate solution. In software-agent deployments, compaction can reduce total spending, although task quality and rewrite-inclusive time depend on the target and compressor. These findings identify reasoning history as one way to reduce future reasoning-token use and motivate model-specific compaction policies evaluated for both efficiency and retained capability.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-02T19:21:48Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <arxiv:journal_ref>Proceedings of the Second Workshop for REsearch on Agent Language Models (REALM 2026), pp. 1213-1228, 2026</arxiv:journal_ref>
    <author>
      <name>Anton Hristov</name>
    </author>
    <arxiv:doi>10.18653/2026.realm-1.84</arxiv:doi>
    <link rel="related" href="https://doi.org/10.18653/2026.realm-1.84" title="doi"/>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03743v1</id>
    <title>EvoMemState: Evolving Memory State for Personal Agents</title>
    <updated>2026-11-02T02:42:04Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03743v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.03743v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Long-term memory enables large language model (LLM) agents to provide continuous personalized assistance by retaining user information across interactions. Existing systems support this process through fact extraction, organization, updating, and retrieval, but largely maintain memories as independent facts. This fact-centric paradigm may overlook how new facts update, constrain, or conflict with earlier ones, while leaving their applicability, role, and priority for the current request implicit. In this paper, we introduce EvoMemState, a state-centric framework that augments persistent facts with two complementary states: Relational States that capture how facts remain valid and relate to one another as memory evolves, and a Reasoning State that determines how these facts should be applied to the current request. Specifically, EvoMemState consists of three key components: (i) Ontology-Guided Memory State Induction extracts Persistent Facts and constructs Relational States that maintain their validity, scope, and evolving relations. (ii) RL-Based State Evolution internalizes memory-use experience into a policy through reinforcement learning to generate a request-specific Reasoning State. (iii) State-Aware Reasoning then combines the relevant facts with their Relational States and the generated Reasoning State to guide the personal agent. Extensive experiments on long-term dialogue and memory-use benchmarks demonstrate consistent improvements over existing memory systems in retrieval completeness and memory-grounded response accuracy. Our code and implementation details are available at https://github.com/XMUDeepLIT/EvoMemState.git.</summary>
    <category term="cs.IR" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-02T02:42:04Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.IR"/>
    <author>
      <name>Zhishang Xiang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yunbo Tang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Zhongqiao Liu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Junkai Chen</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Zerui Chen</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Qingguo Hu</name>
    </author>
