  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05593v1</id>
    <title>GaussianRenderFormer: Transformer-based Rendering of 3D Gaussian Splats</title>
    <updated>2026-11-03T20:26:14Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05593v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.05593v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Can a neural network learn to render 3D Gaussian splats on its own, with no rasterizer in the renderer? We present GaussianRenderFormer, a transformer that takes a set of Gaussians and a camera and outputs the image directly. Each Gaussian becomes one token in a view-independent encoder, and a view-dependent decoder turns small patches of camera rays into pixels by attending to those tokens. Trained on 26,820 Objaverse objects pruned to 20,000 Gaussians each, GaussianRenderFormer renders 300 unseen objects at 38.08 dB PSNR against the full, unpruned splats, while rasterizing the same pruned input reaches 44.02 dB. The gap is not one of generalization, and our diagnostics are consistent with a bottleneck in the view decoder: telling it the projected footprint of each Gaussian, which it could in principle infer, adds 1.4 dB. Under-training is not ruled out. Code, trained models and videos: https://svlwoof.github.io/gaussianrenderformer</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.GR" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-03T20:26:14Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Under review at TMLR. 32 pages. Project page: https://svlwoof.github.io/gaussianrenderformer</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Shahaf Valencia Levy</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Sagie Benaim</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2609.37820v2</id>
    <title>Information Games: Strategic Crowding and Firm Repositioning in Language-Model Space</title>
    <updated>2026-11-05T17:56:23Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.37820v2" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.37820v2" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>When firms respond to new market opportunities, their capacity choices alter the future discounted free cash flows available to both competitors and investors. In competitive markets, these investment decisions publicized in news articles and analyst reports, measuring the changes in capital structure, product mix, firm leadership, risk exposure and operating procedure made by the firm. Modern search engines rely on Large Language Models to measure the relevancy of source documents to given search query. These measurements let researchers represent these news articles as clouds of dense vectors whose information geometry reflects the dissimilarity of firms over time. Building on Xu et al. (2022), we develop a model of costly competitive adjustment in which firms allocate capacity in which firms make firm capacity adjustment towards free cash flow shocks. Using a novel sandwich estimator, we analyse the sign and magnitude of this predicted adjustment using multi-agent simulation, and validate our findings on a sample of news and analyst reports from Nasdaq-listed companies between 2018-2022.</summary>
    <category term="q-fin.CP" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="econ.GN" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-09-29T15:30:02Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="q-fin.CP"/>
    <author>
      <name>Marcus Gawronsky</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Chun-Sung Huang</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08089v1</id>
    <title>SAE Interprets MoE: Sparse AutoEncoders Reveal That MoE Experts Exhibit Human-Interpretable Fine-Grained Specialization</title>
    <updated>2026-11-05T14:35:08Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08089v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.08089v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) models route each token to a few of many experts, yet what an individual expert is responsible for remains unsettled. Existing analyses count how often an expert is selected on domain-labeled corpora, an instrument that fixes the taxonomy in advance, stays coarse, and records selection rather than contribution. We instead study specialization in feature space by training a sparse autoencoder (SAE) on the output of an MoE layer. Since this output is a router-weighted sum of expert outputs and the SAE encoder is affine, the pre-activation of every latent decomposes exactly over the experts. Aggregated over a corpus, this gives each expert a dominant set of latents whose activation it principally drives. On layers 4, 30 and 46 of Qwen3-30B-A3B, contribution is far more concentrated than gating, most latents have only one or two dominators, and the overlap between dominant sets concentrates on a few expert pairs. Auto-interpreting these sets reveals a division of labor below coarse domains, with distinct experts for Java and front-end web code, or for geometry and for arithmetic and polynomials, while the coarse domains themselves take very unequal numbers of experts. Our SAEs and code are available at https://github.com/Bytedance-Seed/SAE-Interprets-MoE.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-05T14:35:08Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Anyuan Zhuo</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Shaohua Zhang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yuan Lin</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Pinyan Lu</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08373v1</id>
    <title>SLA: Scaling Spatial-Language-Action Model via Continual Pre-training</title>
    <updated>2026-11-05T17:52:16Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08373v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.08373v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Reliable robotic manipulation requires both semantic understanding and precise control in three-dimensional space. However, vision-language-action (VLA) foundations largely inherit visual representations from 2D vision-language pre-training, while spatial supervision is often introduced only during task-specific adaptation. Developing spatial understanding as a transferable foundation-model capability therefore calls for learning from broader robot experience. We present SLA, a Spatial-Language-Action Model that scales implicit spatial alignment to continual pre-training and maintains it during downstream post-training. Initialized from a pretrained pi05 policy, SLA jointly optimizes action prediction and alignment between intermediate visual embeddings and spatial representations from a frozen 3D perception model. We curate and unify heterogeneous robot datasets to support spatial learning across diverse embodiments, tasks, and scenes. To accommodate this diversity, we introduce feature-dependent gates that adaptively aggregate visual embeddings from multiple VLA layers for spatial supervision. The teacher further exploits partially available depth and camera calibration alongside RGB observations, enabling effective use of heterogeneous geometric annotations. Probabilistic state dropout encourages reliance on visual cues and mitigates overfitting to demonstration trajectories. All auxiliary components are used only during training, allowing direct reuse of pretrained VLA weights and preserving the original inference architecture.</summary>
    <category term="cs.RO" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-05T17:52:16Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Project page: https://SLA.github.io/ ; Github: https://github.com/OpenHelix-Team/Spatial-Forcing/tree/master/SLA</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.RO"/>
    <author>
      <name>Fuhao Li</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Wenxuan Song</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Qizhou Huang</name>
    </author>
